WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.586
Post-quantum cryptography is not only a research topic.

2
00:00:03.586 --> 00:00:17.931
It is a practical migration issue for any organization that relies on public-key cryptography for key exchange, digital signatures, certificates, software updates, service authentication, or long-lived confidentiality.

3
00:00:17.931 --> 00:00:28.465
Quantum-capable attacks are not something most security teams can observe directly today, but migration work has to start before systems are already under pressure.

4
00:00:28.465 --> 00:00:37.655
NIST standards matter because they give vendors, governments, architects, developers, auditors, and security teams a shared reference point.

5
00:00:37.655 --> 00:00:43.931
Without standards, every organization would have to interpret the algorithm landscape on its own.

6
00:00:43.931 --> 00:00:55.660
Standards reduce uncertainty by naming the algorithms, parameter sets, interfaces, and expected security categories that can be used as the basis for products and migration planning.

7
00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:59.994
It helps to separate several stages that are often blended together.

8
00:00:59.994 --> 00:01:05.373
Algorithm selection means NIST has chosen an algorithm family for standardization.

9
00:01:05.373 --> 00:01:09.407
Standardization means the formal standard has been published.

10
00:01:09.407 --> 00:01:13.666
Implementation means libraries and products actually support it.

11
00:01:13.666 --> 00:01:18.746
Validation means an implementation can be tested through recognized programs.

12
00:01:18.746 --> 00:01:24.051
Deployment means real systems have adopted it safely, interoperably, and at scale.

13
00:01:24.051 --> 00:01:29.580
Security practitioners do not need to become cryptographers to use the standards well.

14
00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:37.648
They do need to understand what each standard is for, where it may appear in real systems, and what migration questions it raises.

15
00:01:37.648 --> 00:01:46.464
The practical job is to connect standards to inventory, vendor readiness, protocol choices, testing, key management, and staged rollout.
